union task_kind =
    | Epic :: task_kind
    | Feature :: task_kind
    | Story :: task_kind
    | TaskKind :: task_kind

union priority =
    | P0 :: priority | P1 :: priority | P2 :: priority | P3 :: priority | P4 :: priority | P5 :: priority

union difficulty =
    | D0 :: difficulty | D1 :: difficulty | D2 :: difficulty | D3 :: difficulty | D4 :: difficulty
    | D5 :: difficulty | D6 :: difficulty | D7 :: difficulty | D8 :: difficulty

union task_status =
    | Planned :: task_status | Active :: task_status | Blocked :: task_status | Paused :: task_status | Done :: task_status

union agile_item =
    | Task :: string * string * task_kind * priority * difficulty * u32 * task_status * string * string * string * string -> agile_item

union task_load =
    | Light :: task_load | Medium :: task_load | Heavy :: task_load

union task_assignee =
    | Agent :: task_assignee | Pair :: task_assignee | User :: task_assignee

union planned_estimate =
    | Unestimated :: planned_estimate | Minutes :: u32 -> planned_estimate

union planned_load =
    | LoadUnset :: planned_load | Load :: task_load -> planned_load

union planned_assignee =
    | Unassigned :: planned_assignee | Assigned :: task_assignee -> planned_assignee

union task_planning =
    | Planning :: string * planned_estimate * planned_load * planned_assignee -> task_planning

inl eoie_dogfood () = Task ("EOIE-DOGFOOD", "Use eoie for our agile backlog and tool runs with the compiler configured, and fix every friction so it beats Claude Code tools: (1) unbounded/renewing lease on local machines, configurable, no per-lease reports (user); (2) help and inspection never blocked by an expired lease, accept --help on every verb; (3) agile list renders a table (id, P, D, %, status, title) instead of raw .spi lines; (4) agile status shows local times and remaining minutes instead of unix ms; (5) agile add to create tasks; (6) agile init scaffolds a root (state/, package.spiproj with agile+prompt); (7) actionable package-graph errors (|core- in state package was rejected without a reason); (8) record-preflight must not demand eoie release files (history, ratings, bench, migration) from a plain backlog root; done 2026-10-08: (1) EOIE_LEASE_POLICY=renewing, (5) agile add, (9) agile edit, (3) in progress; acceptance bar: every verb beats its pwsh counterpart: elapsed time, counts, bytes, exit code, stderr digest, cache hits in every result; aligned human output plus --json; diagnostics that run the obvious intent instead of only suggesting it; findings (9) agile edit for descriptions/notes; (10) eoie help <capability> should show proxy help instead of exit 2; (11) normalize path separators in outputs (root\\\\.github/x); (12) say expected vs received argument count on bad arguments; (13) dev.ps1 should accept module names (agile_state_runtime_policy_domain) as well as package names; (14) a failed cargo check must surface the first rustc error, not only 'Cargo rejected regenerated EOIE owners'; (15) fs-search must accept '.' as the root itself; 08:2x done: (3) agile list table sorted by priority with status counts and elapsed_ms, (9) agile edit; new findings (16) text-replace needs --all/--expect N for multi-match edits; (17) EOIE_LEASE_POLICY should also come from the eoie config file (shells started before the user env var miss it); (18) fs-remove prints nothing on success; (19) list header % column off by one; (20) inspection (text-replace check) still lease-blocked; 08:4x done: (4) agile status shows phase, policy and relative times (started=2h49m_ago) beside the raw unix fields, (17) lease policy from <config>/eoie/lease-policy (this machine: renewing) with EOIE_LEASE_POLICY still first, (19) list header aligned, (20) text-replace check / *-preview modes are inspections (effect key verb:sub:mode, lookup moved from rust_global into Spiral crate agile_lease_terms); contract tests isolate EOIE_LEASE_POLICY and EOIE_CONFIG_HOME; 08:5x done: (2)+(10) --help/-h anywhere after a verb or subcommand and help <capability> answer with exit 0 before the lease guard (Spiral help_request union in command_spec_domain); left: (6) agile init, (7) package-graph errors, (8) record-preflight, (11) path separators, (12) arg-count diagnostics, (13) dev.ps1 module names, (14) cargo error surfacing, (15) fs-search ., (16) text-replace --all, (18) fs-remove receipt, enriched outputs everywhere; 08:5x done: (6) agile init <root> scaffolds state/package.spiproj + the typed agile.spi schema (Spiral crate agile_backlog_scaffold, Renew effect like begin); init -> begin -> add -> check verified on a fresh root; 09:1x done: (12) agile arity errors name the expected shape and received operand count, (16) text-replace migrated from the legacy rust_global arm to Spiral crate proxy_text_replacement: --expect N / --all, typed mode/expectation unions, rooted path via rust_std_fs (finding 11 for this verb), atomic replace + readback, receipt with matches/expected/bytes/elapsed_ms; 09:2x done: (18) fs-remove migrated to Spiral crate proxy_file_removal with a receipt (path, kind file|symlink, bytes, elapsed_ms) and typed rejections (missing, directory -> use fs-remove-tree, root escape), (15) fs-search <root> . searches the root (Spiral search_scope union; . stays rejected for mutating verbs so fs-remove-tree . cannot wipe a root); left: (7) package-graph errors, (8) record-preflight, (13) dev.ps1 module names, (14) cargo error surfacing, enriched receipts on the remaining legacy verbs; 09:4x done: (13) dev.ps1 accepts crate directory names and underscored names (resolved to Cargo package names, unknown names still rejected), (14) a failed cargo check now puts the first rustc error block into the thrown message; left: (7) package-graph errors, (8) record-preflight wording for plain backlogs, enriched receipts on the remaining legacy verbs; 09:5x done: proxy run/command failures are no longer reported as usage errors: a child that exits N makes eoie exit N with a plain error after its receipt (Spiral crate proxy_failure_classification: ArgumentFailure | ChildProcessFailure), misuse still exits 2 with usage; evidence renewed ready=True at 09:41 (before this change: renew again at end of day); 10:1x done: agile list <root> [filter] --json emits one JSON document {tasks[], shown, filter, counts{planned,active,blocked,paused,done}, elapsed_ms} (listing_format union; status census split into Spiral crate agile_task_census and scan helpers moved into agile_task_text to stay under the 1000-line crate gate); quotes and backslashes round-trip; raw tabs in titles are not escaped yet (spi_escaped lacks \\t); 10:5x done: (7) the compiler host now says which packages failed and why a |core- packages: entry fails (SPIRAL_COMPILER_PACKAGE_DIR or a deps/The-Spiral-Language clone); verified: with the variable set the same backlog checks clean (spiral dev24); 10:4x done: (8) agile check prints \"records catalog=v1 present=0/4 missing=... (agile check does not need them)\" from the new typed crate agile_record_catalog (RecordCatalogComplete | RecordCatalogPartial), replacing the ready=0 text in the rust_global blob; spi_escaped escapes tabs; eoie_agile_state.spi unchanged vs HEAD; 195/195; 11:0x receipts: fs-copy migrated whole into typed crate proxy_file_copy (elapsed_ms, precise rooted errors kept); agile set receipt from typed crate agile_update_receipt (bytes_before/after + elapsed_ms like agile edit); parallel-search accepts . as the whole root; workspace_primitives.rooted shares proxy argument + root confinement (fs-remove/text-replace regenerated byte-identical); 195/195. Next: parallel-search receipt elapsed, hit-limit truncation instead of abort (needs inspection_collect migrated out of rust_global); 12:5x parallel-search: new typed receipt crate proxy_search_receipt shows serial_ms/parallel_ms/elapsed_ms; it exposed a 111 s search of eoie/src (src/target = 61,538 files, 11.7 GB of build output); the search walk now skips build output (typed search_directory: .git, node_modules, CACHEDIR.TAG or .rustc_info.json) and the receipt says excludes=...: 1.5 s, same 7 hits; hash-tree/manifest walks unchanged; 195/195; 13:1x parallel-search over max-hits truncates deterministically instead of aborting (each subtree keeps limit+1, serial/parallel parity holds) and the typed receipt says hits=N truncated=true found_at_least=N+1 (hit_budget union); fs-search, which has no receipt, keeps an explicit error naming EOIE_SEARCH_MAX_HITS; 195/195; 16:37 evidence renewed after the afternoon batch (chain.ps1 -SkipBuild, label r1008b): cold renewal 119/119 chains, agile check --compiler exit 0, closeout applied, strict preflight -RequireReady ready=True diagnostics=0 (the pre-closeout gates rejected the stale Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock receipts the new crates changed, as expected); 17:0x new verb proxy command-output <root> <timeout-ms> <program> [args...]: prints the child stdout/stderr directly, then a typed receipt (crate proxy_command_output: ok|child-failed status elapsed_ms stdout_bytes stderr_bytes) and propagates the child exit code; replaces the command-capture + read-receipt two-step for git reads; registered in usage/help/lease (nested eoie child at index 4) and legacy surface; lesson: proxy_direct_route_names is positional (route code = index), legacy-dispatched verbs stay out of it; 195/195; 17:04 evidence renewed again after command-output (label r1008d): cold renewal, agile check --compiler exit 0, strict preflight ready=True diagnostics=0; 17:4x parallel-search aborted on the first symlink child (refusing symlink: dice\\deps\\polyglot), so whole-repo searches of dice/polyglot failed; symlink children are now a typed SymlinkEntry (not descended, receipt excludes=...,symlinks) while a symlinked start path still errors; dice searches 0.2 s; 195/195; eoie changed again, evidence renewal due; 18:16 evidence renewed after the symlink fix: strict preflight ready=True diagnostics=0; 10-11 21:3x landed the fs-search receipt (the gap named at 13:1x): proxy_search_receipt/search_receipt.spi grew fs_search_receipt_text plus its eoie_fs_search_receipt_text export, parallel_search_contract/search_terminal.spi times inspection_search (started before collect, found captured before the consuming loop) and prints after the hits, dev.ps1 -Publish emitted 2/2, passed cargo check and its tests and published the 2 changed owners, the eoie-cli debug build exited 0 carrying the downstream crates with it, and a real run on a scratch root prints eoie proxy fs-search ok hits=2 excludes=.git,node_modules,build-output,symlinks elapsed_ms=3 with exit 0 (the first draft printed a double space after ok, fixed and re-regenerated before the recorded run); receipts now cover agile set, command-output, parallel-search, text-replace, fs-remove, fs-copy and fs-search, while fs-slice, fs-context, hash and hash-tree still print bare output in search.rs and parallel-manifest has an ok-line but no elapsed_ms; 4 files changed unstaged (search_receipt.spi, search_terminal.spi, search_receipt.rs, search.rs), cargo test --workspace green at 21:40 (214 suites, 178 passed + 17 ignored = 195, 0 failed, exit 0), and the measured evidence debt at 21:45: test-strict-preflight.ps1 reports ready=False diagnostics=3 with bundle error evidence currentness evidence tree identity mismatch path=src (expected dbb103fa..., observed 44b84681...) plus ColdProofV4 census drift expected=21544 observed=21550, the drift being exactly the 6 spiral lines this change added, so evidence renewal via the formal-receipts-renew and census-gate path stays due; the two published .rs diffs also carry regeneration drift these files had not yet picked up, enum variants renamed readable (US0_0 to US0_AllHitsShown, and to US0_SourceDirectory, BuildOutputDirectory, VersionControlDirectory, PackageInstallDirectory, SymlinkEntry) plus new impl Default blocks, only 3 of the 42 added search.rs lines being the receipt itself, all of it compiling and covered by the green suite; 2026-10-12 05:31Z: fs-slice fs-context hash hash-tree get receipts and parallel-manifest gets elapsed_ms via Spiral emitters in proxy_search_receipt (hash receipts on stderr so stdout stays the digest); build.ps1 -Test 178 passed 0 failed 17 ignored; dev.ps1 splits comma package lists; 2026-10-12 06:10Z: help proxy now lists formal-receipts-renew and coverage-register; build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts 195 passed 0 failed; renewal chain (agile begin, renew-cold-rebuild -Apply, agile check --compiler, staged census + release-closeout apply) gives strict preflight ready=True diagnostics=0; 2026-10-12 06:48Z: agile edit title-append now joins with \"; \" unless the text starts with punctuation (field rendering split into typed crate agile_field_rendering, which also brings agile_task_revision back under the 1000-line gate); rooted paths print native separators; fs-read gets a stderr receipt (bytes, shown, truncated, no_final_newline, elapsed_ms), fs-list a stdout receipt, fs-write and fs-remove-tree elapsed_ms (crate proxy_file_receipt); text-replace usage names --all/--expect; renewal chain scripted as compiler-contracts/renew-strict-evidence.ps1; 2026-10-12 08:16Z: new inspection agile show <root> <id> (crate agile_task_inspection: full unescaped title, deps, tags, receipt); renew-strict-evidence.ps1 now renews the owner growth receipt (new crates agile_field_rendering, proxy_file_receipt, agile_task_inspection) and ran end to end: build.ps1 -Test -CompilerContracts 195 passed 0 failed, strict preflight ready=True diagnostics=0, eoie.exe cb2b3aed; a first run hit the 15 s cold owner budget under load (16.7 s), passing at -ParallelChains 2; 2026-10-12 09:25Z: fs-list moved whole into typed crate proxy_directory_listing, fs-copy-tree gained elapsed_ms, renewal retries the cold budget at fewer chains; 195/0, strict preflight ready=True, eoie.exe ef711a26; 2026-10-12 10:20Z: agile show --json, full proxy help list, renewal stops early on unrenewable diagnostics; final eoie.exe f2d681ff, 195/0, ready=True; 2026-10-12 11:29Z: fs-read moved whole into typed crate proxy_file_reading (streamed, byte-exact), fs-search truncates like parallel-search; 195/0, ready=True, eoie.exe 2a3eeee3; 2026-10-12 12:05Z: fs-read now fails (exit 2) on stream or flush errors; final eoie.exe ce371e74, 195/0, ready=True; 2026-10-12 08:17 lane closed by its agent: receipts, agile show, fs-read/fs-list typed crates, renew-strict-evidence.ps1, strict preflight ready=True (cee3a96); follow-ups in EOIE-REGEN-DRIFT", Epic, P0, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "eoie,dogfood,agile", "current", "current")
inl readable_generated_names () = Task ("READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES", "Generated code as human readable as possible on every backend: functions, unions, union cases, layouts and closures named after their Spiral source (join point, method and type names) instead of method12, U3, closure7; the numeric index only on name collisions or where inlining and join points leave no name; local names inside bodies derived from the Spiral bindings where possible. Performance first: naming must not change codegen shape or runtime cost; oracle outputs keep agreeing while residual hashes change once; build the naming as an onion layer (our own naming pass the backends consult) so upstream codegen diffs stay small; 2026-10-08 investigation: names already exist: LangEnv.join_point_method_key_names / join_point_closure_key_names map each join point key to its Spiral name (used for EJP diagnostics) but PartEvalResult does not expose them; 34 naming sites (method{tag}/closure{tag}) across ~12 backends, C/F#/Python/Cuda are upstream sections; plan: expose the tables in PartEvalResult (both cores), one GeneratedNames section maps tag -> sanitized source name (+ suffix on collision, deterministic in tag order), backends call it; upstream sections need a one-call hook each (decide with the upstream onion move); then one reviewed re-bless; 2026-10-08 14:2x correction: the names already flow: single-flight stores jp_name in each join_point_method entry, hopac in JpMethodBodyCellReadyPayload, and C/Cpp (upstream) already emit them (loop, score, derivative...; anonymous join points stay method{n}); the other backends dropped them with | body, range, _ ->; slice 1: generatedMethodName (CodegenRust section: [A-Za-z0-9_] sanitized, {name}_{tag} so it cannot meet v{n}/closure{n}/US0_ names; Gleam lowercased) used by Rust, Delphi, Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, TypeScript, Python through a per-codegen tag->name map; dev review: 2,018 jobs, no regression, DISAGREE 0, 286 changed rows proven identical after name normalization, the rest Bend prelude/helper names derived from method names; F# left: CodegenFsharp precedes generatedMethodName and codegenMethodBody (move the helper earlier), and hopac F# drops methodName at the cell classification (167864/167872, CodegenJpMethodBodyCellReady has no name; its codegen uses concurrent collections); 15:2x blessed (dev28 deployed): 2,018 rows, review = bless run row for row, 338 changed rows all renames (fn method0 -> fn fib_0; stems seen: loop, score, derivative, make_star, regex_compare, canonical_derivative); F# in both cores implemented (helper moved to the end of CodegenUtils; hopac carries (range, sourceName) through the generic cell unions), build + review next; 16:0x F# blessed (dev29): 41 rows changed, 39 proven rename-only vs HEAD (2 format_any carry the blessed renumbering), renamed .fsx run in dotnet fsi exit 0 (the harness only compiles F#); now every backend names functions after the Spiral source (C/Cpp {name}{tag}, the rest {name}_{tag}); upstream export: CodegenC/CodegenFsharp/CodegenUtils clean, CodegenPython 2 conflicts (section already differs from the fork there); next slices: closures (closure{n}) and union/layout names; closures next: single-flight stores names only for methods (165470); closure entries are (domain_args, body), hopac keeps closure names only in join_point_closure_key_names (diagnostics); closure naming needs PartEval to store a name in the closure entry (upstream section) before any backend can use it; 18:0x found by the dice regression build: join points named after operators (e.g. |>) sanitized to underscore-only names (fn ___9, def ___3); generatedMethodName now falls back to method{tag} unless the source name has a letter or digit; suite review all parity (no sample has operator-named join points), deployed dev30 (both cores), dice outputs regenerated; 01:4x union case names slice: generatedCaseNames/generatedCaseSuffixes (CodegenUtils, next to generatedMethodName: sanitized Spiral case name, index only on collision or when nothing readable remains) used by Rust (enum variants US0_Some), Zig and Delphi (constructor functions), TypeScript (case types and constructors); union type names stay US0/UH0 and runtime tags stay numeric; dev review single-flight-20261009-013031: DISAGREE 0, 126 changed rows (Rust 34, Zig 35, Delphi 32, TypeScript 25) all rename-only after case-name normalization, natives agree; left: Lua (its case name doubles as a runtime string tag Uh0i1), Lean/Gleam/Bend (no per-case constructor names), upstream C/F#/Python, closures; 02:44 Lua slice blessed (Uh0_Cons, incl. runtime tag strings; 32 Lua rows rename-only; main oracle 2183 rows, repro 0); Lean (U0.Cons) + Gleam (Uh0Cons) slice in review; 03:34 Lean (U0.Cons) + Gleam (Uh0Cons) blessed (57 rows rename-only; main oracle 2183, repro 0); F# slice (both cores, UnionTag as a match) in review; 04:25 F# blessed (UH0_Cons in both cores; UnionTag now a match, was a Map that could not compile for cases with fields; 47 rows rename-only, fsi compile-checked; main oracle 2183, repro 0); C + Python (tag-keyed names) in review; left: C++/CUDA struct names, Bend, closures; 05:20 C + Python blessed (tag-keyed generatedCaseNameOfTag; 53 rows rename-only, every backend still agrees with the renamed C oracle; repro 0); Bend (U0_Cons) in review; left: C++/CUDA structs (Union0_0), closures; 06:15 Bend blessed (U0_Cons; 27 rows rename-only, all agree; repro 0). Union case names now readable in Rust, Zig, Delphi, TypeScript, Lua, Lean, Gleam, F# (both cores), C, Python, Bend. Left: closure names on backends other than Rust. 2026-10-10 C++/CUDA case structs use the Spiral case name (Union0_Hit, Union0_Miss, Union0_Nil, Union0_Cons). The outer type stays Union{i}, the field stays case{n}, and the runtime tag stays the number. single-flight-20261010-173754, hopac-20261010-174528, and single-flight-20261010-174600 agree with C, DISAGREE 0. Not blessed. Rust, Kotlin, Swift and TypeScript closures from a named inl binding emit stem_c tag (fn f_c0, fun f_c0, func f_c0, function f_c0); anonymous closures stay closure n. Rust: single-flight-20261011-070642, hopac-20261011-071623, and restore single-flight-20261011-072251 agree with C, DISAGREE 0 (C and Rust). Kotlin, Swift and TypeScript: single-flight-20261011-073934, hopac-20261011-095228, and restore single-flight-20261011-101905 each ran the C+Kotlin+Swift+TypeScript examples suite (1052 ran, 10 build-fail, oracle agree 609, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, script exit 0), and the restore is row for row identical to single-flight-20261011-073934 (0 of 1,092 rows differ). Zig, Lean, Bend, Gleam, Lua, Wasm and Delphi closures: single-flight-20261011-122112, hopac-20261011-123041, and restore single-flight-20261011-124049 each ran the C+Delphi+Zig+Lean+Bend+Gleam+Lua+Wasm examples suite (1650 jobs, 12 build-fail, oracle agree 1140, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, regressed 0, broken 0, script exit 0), and the restore is row for row identical to single-flight-20261011-122112 (0 of 1,650 rows differ). Left: closures on the remaining backends (C, Python, C++/CUDA). Not blessed. 2026-10-11 F# (both cores) and Python closures use the source name: {stem}_c{tag} (let rec f_c0 / def f_c0), closure{n} or Closure{n} otherwise. F# through a closure_names memo (ConcurrentDictionary hopac / Dictionary single-flight) filled from closureSourceName at the closure join point (JpBodyOwnerIdentity.body jp_body in hopac, snd jp_body in single-flight); Python through a name field on ClosureRec taken from the source name codegenClosureBody already returns. Python is natively compared to C; F# is not (test.ps1 excludes Fsharp from the native jobs), so F# is compile-checked by the suite and 16 closure samples probe-run in fsi exit 0. single-flight-20261011-140929, hopac-20261011-141813, and restore single-flight-20261011-142157 each ran C+Fsharp+Python examples -Native: 814 jobs, oracle agree 19, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, regressed 0, broken 0, script exit 0; the restore reproduces single-flight-20261011-140929 row for row (0 of 814 rows differ). The only residual changes are the closure names (F# portable_convert_parse; Python portable_convert_parse, ts_closure_capture, ts_closure_return, all still agreeing with C). 2026-10-11 C closures use the source name: {stem}_c{tag} when the closure comes from a named inl binding (int32_t f_c0), closure{n} otherwise. codegenClosureBody already returned the source name (the C section dropped it with _ before); the ClosureRecC carries a name field and the closure body function is generatedClosureName name tag. The env struct Closure{i}, the factory ClosureCreate{i} and the two decrefs stay tag-keyed, exactly as Zig keeps ClosureEnv{tag}/closureCreate{tag}. single-flight-20261011-152506, hopac-20261011-153643, and restore single-flight-20261011-154343 each ran C+Rust+Delphi+Python examples -Native: 1276 jobs, 1196 native ran, 12 build-fail, oracle agree 745, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, regressed 0, broken 0, script exit 0; the restore reproduces single-flight-20261011-152506 row for row (0 of 1276 rows differ). The only residual changes are the C closure body names on the 99 samples that define a closure (closure_apply ClosureMethod0 -> f_c0, closure_curry -> closure1); every backend still agrees with C. Left: closures on C++/CUDA; 10-11 20:5x scoped so the call is decidable: generatedClosureName (spiral_compiler.fs 167281, yields stem_cTag and closureTag for anonymous) is called from 15 sites covering C (179175, cname for the closure method) and Python (181197) plus the middle backends, but the CodegenCpp.fs upstream section (header at 179552, pinned @ 0cb0fda0 host_cpp_and_cuda_backend) calls it zero times and both its backends name closures as tag-only struct types: 179881 constructs local Closure{tag}, 180004 news Closure{tag} behind a ClosureBase{tag} csptr, 180295 emits struct Closure{tag} : public ClosureBase{tag}, 180321 the device constructor, 180329 the device destructor. Unlike C, where the readable name attaches to the method while the struct stays Closure{tag}, C++ and CUDA emit operator() (180305-180306) so there is no method name to rename: only the struct type is a candidate, it must stay paired with a ClosureBase{tag} built from the independent cfun tag space (180294), it sits in a section the upstream sync replaces wholesale so every differing line needs a ws: marker, and renaming churns every emitted C++ and CUDA file; design call for the user. Not blessed.; 07:48 design call made (main, autonomous, per the 10-13 upstream-shape decision): C++/CUDA closure structs stay tag-keyed (Closure{tag} paired with ClosureBase{tag}); that section is replaced wholesale by the upstream sync and C++/CUDA use operator(), so no method name exists to rename and the churn would touch every emitted C++/CUDA file for little readability; every other backend already emits {stem}_c{tag}", Epic, P1, D6, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,codegen,readability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_readable_generated_names () = Planning ("READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl node_to_bun () = Task ("NODE-TO-BUN", "Use bun only, no node: spiral harness TypeScript tier (env.ps1 Node, test.ps1, native-shims/run_main.mjs worker + stack size), Kino toolchain.ex TypeScript cells, CI setup-node in spiral/polyglot/dice/i574n.github gh-pages.yml and gitlab node -v; verify every TypeScript row agrees with the C oracle under bun first; 22:1x closed: full suite single-flight-20261008-215335 runs all 117 TypeScript rows under bun, 117 agree with C; CI workflows in spiral/polyglot/dice/i574n.github use oven-sh/setup-bun only; remaining node references are vendored third-party (Fable, dotnet-interactive, hyperui, VS Code plugin) or untracked dist/ build copies", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "bun,typescript,ci,kino", "current", "current")
inl ci_spiral_rust_run_check () = Task ("CI-SPIRAL-RUST-RUN-CHECK", "spiral Windows CI: apps/spiral/build.ps1 run check `spiral rust` exits 1 with no output; passes locally; stderr now printed, read it on the next push and fix; 07:03 push: run check passes (RUST-OK apps/spiral); next failure apps/wasm cargo via bash on Windows", TaskKind, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,spiral,windows", "current", "current")
inl ci_dice_e2e () = Task ("CI-DICE-E2E", "dice ubuntu e2e: playwright webServer ran through /bin/sh which expanded $job; named-job fix verified locally, confirm on the next push and walk the later steps; 07:03 push failed earlier at lib/spiral/common.fsx missing (fixed by FSX-FREE); 22:0x CI run 37854297989 (18:34 push): build (ubuntu-24.04) success, so the e2e fix holds; the windows job failure is the WSL contract path tracked in CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,dice,linux", "current", "current")
inl bless_byte_to_i32_switch () = Task ("BLESS-BYTE-TO-I32-SWITCH", "Suite, review and bless the samples/core byte_to_i32 BackendSwitch (C identical modulo variable numbering) and the comment-free samples", TaskKind, P1, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,oracle", "current", "current")
inl bend_backend () = Task ("BEND-BACKEND", "Bend 2 backend named Bend, Windows-native toolchain (bun from source), as stable as Zig/Lean/Rust/C: IO codegen, unions, tuples, arrays, strings, closures, 8/16-bit ints, harness list; 2026-10-08 09:3x: 115/133 agree (was 72), 0 wrong: mutable aliased arrays as a one-slot Chan cell holding a power-of-two Bend Array (per-element-type helper set, O(log n) peek/swap, bounds abort 3), nested arrays, unions holding arrays/functions declared is Type, closures as lambdas over closure defs; harness Bend list 116 + Known bounds row; left: floats (Bend 2 has F32 only, no formatter), i64/u8, 2 raw-macro while samples, 1 macro sample; 10:1x blessed: review run and bless run identical on 2004 rows (6 FOR-D rows filtered by design), oracle now holds the 116 Bend rows; 10:2x: F32 (Bend F32 ops/compare, decimal literals with F32.neg/division for negatives, inf, nan, signed i32<->f32 helpers) -> 119/133 agree; deployed dev23; harness Bend list 120; left: f64 (no Bend F64; emulating it in F32 would change results), i64/u8, 2 mutable-layout samples, 2 raw-macro while samples, 1 raw-macro sample; 10:4x blessed C+Bend (350 rows; review and bless runs identical; the 116 earlier Bend residuals changed only by the 23-line F32 prelude); oracle holds 120 Bend rows; 13:2x coverage probe (test.ps1 -Suite examples -Native -Probe Bend, 229 Bend rows, outputs in the scratch dir): 119 agree (exactly the listed set) + 1 known, DISAGREE 0; the 108 others: 33 raw C macros/ABI, 16 negative samples rejected everywhere, 23 BackendSwitch records without a Bend key (while_* and native_cube_* assign through !!!!BackendSwitch: needs mutable locals; Rust/Python-specific samples; lib functions without Bend arms), f64 x5 (Bend 2 has only Nat/U32/F32: no faithful f64), u64 x2 (native_closure_union_rec, rust_static_closure_chain: Nat mod 2^64), the integer zoo ts_int_wrap (u8/i8 via U32 masking), layouts x4 (heap/mut records); next by unlock size: mutable locals for while/assignment, then u64 via Nat, then small ints; 17:5x u64 decision input: Bend 2 Nat is unary (type Nat: Zero | Succ, Nat.add/sub recurse per unit), so u64 via Nat is not viable; it needs a (U32, U32) two-word encoding with carry add/sub/mul and 64/32 long division, for 2 unlockable samples: parked", Feature, P1, D7, 92u32, Active, "", "backend,bend", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_bend_backend () = Planning ("BEND-BACKEND", Minutes 240u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl compiler_upstream_onion () = Task ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", "Move the compiler into the upstream fork's repo with our changes as onion layers over mrakgr's files (PR-ready), one file per backend, splitter adapted for parallel builds; 2026-10-08 first upstream-ready patch: CodegenC (user globals reach C output; (void)x in empty decref bodies, 211 -Wunused-parameter -> 0 apart from one stale output), made line-local so scripts/upstream.py exports it cleanly (4 hunks): python scripts/upstream.py export deps/The-Spiral-Language HEAD <out> --since HEAD writes The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs; opening the PR on the fork is the user's call; 18:2x readable-names + C patch material now durable at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\upstream-pr\\names-and-c (CodegenC, CodegenFsharp, CodegenPython, CodegenUtils); both CodegenPython conflicts resolved by intent (fork already carries the string option source name in join_point_method); upstream.py export fix: an addition after the last line of a section goes to the end of the target file (it was spliced between codegen_env and its members); generatedMethodName uses System.Char (CodegenUtils opens only System.Text); verified by a differential build of the fork (unpatched vs patched, external includes restored from history): 0 patch-only errors, with a canary type error proving fsc reaches the codegen files past the unrelated lib/fsharp/Common.fs errors; 21:1x CodegenC now emits the backend standard includes before user globals (Seq.append globals env.globals |> Seq.distinct): user globals using bool/size_t no longer precede <stdbool.h>; dev review: only the include order changed in the 2 samples with C globals (Lean exit=-1 rows were load kills, parity alone); deployed dev32 both cores; patch material CodegenC.fs refreshed (export --since 98492a63, since the 18:34 commit moved HEAD past the names work); 19:4x two fresh candidate exports exist for the next curation pass, both exit 0 against the fork clone at polyglot/deps/The-Spiral-Language: names-and-c-194526 (export --since HEAD, so only tonight's uncommitted edits, 45634 bytes, carries the closure void-cast fix at lines 616-617 but not the committed globals fix) and names-and-c-full-194526 (export --since 98492a63, the documented PR base, 46185 bytes, carries all three: the void-cast fix, the Seq.append globals env.globals fix from C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED and generatedClosureName from the readable-names work); both report conflicts the fork has since moved into (14 and 20 hunks, mostly CodegenCpp plus 2-3 CodegenC hunks in the closure and string-compare areas), so the curated 10-08 names-and-c material stays canonical and opening the PR remains the user's call; 2026-10-12 split: non-defacing changes go upstream as a PR series (test-block parser skip, canonical package dirs, DeadPureLetElision, hermetic BackendSwitch, the new backends, bug fixes), each with its sample rows; edition-3 syntax stays fork-only (SPIRAL3-EDITIONS); 08:13 input from the water-oil re-shape probe: to make shared Graph/ServerUtils/WDiff upstream-shaped, hopac's supervisor must stop requiring interned path ids in shared code (42 compile errors when ServerUtils takes single-flight's text); plan: hopac keeps ids in its own layer and converts at the boundary of the shared functions; 2026-10-13 22:59 the spiral-split gear splitter (scripts/gear-dev.ps1, bench-split.ps1, build-splitter.ps1, tmp/splitter, lanes/splitter) was removed at the user's request: idle since early October (234 runs Sep 26-Oct 1, then 21), built for gpt5parallel's 159k lines; use build.ps1 -Configuration Debug -CoreSource <copy> for candidates; 2026-10-13 23:41 user decision: after hopacsota promotion (HOPACSOTA-P6); the splitter it mentions was deleted 2026-10-13", Epic, P1, D8, 10u32, Planned, "HOPACSOTA-P6", "compiler,upstream", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_compiler_upstream_onion () = Planning ("COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION", Minutes 3000u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl hopac_speed () = Task ("HOPAC-SPEED", "Measure hopac vs single-flight (real-work rows 3-22x slower, fresh process per job), split startup from evaluation, profile and iterate to single-flight speed; stats 2026-10-08 09:5x (probe.ps1, fresh process per compile, F# backend, mean of runs 2-3, net = total - frontier_hello baseline): startup sf 1.0s / hopac 1.45s; net eval brzozowski 0.65s / 0.8s (1.2x), zeta 0.95 / 1.4 (1.5x), omniledger 2.65 / 5.0 (1.9x), lean_cic 1.8 / 5.5 (3.1x), spiral_proves 1.5 / 4.65 (3.1x); the suite-level 126x median compares warm single-flight workers with fresh hopac processes and overstates the gap; next: profile lean_cic; profile lean_cic hopac (probe -Profile 12, SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET=1), innermost compiler frames: term 36%, installBridge 7.4% (thread-local tail-result bridge save/restore per declared JP step), ty_core_impl 6.8%, buildFreshApplyState 3.2%, DiagSidecar.emitReentryKeySample 2.6% (runs even when quiet: ConditionalWeakTable + 2 AddOrUpdate per re-entry; counts may feed hot-key fallback, check before gating), DiagJson runner/loop/emitRecord 5.5% (background thread, CPU not wall), EvalWorklist replay tick 2.3%; candidates: gate sidecar sampling when quiet if counts are diagnostic-only, make the bridge scope a struct/stack instead of ThreadLocal round trips; each change needs an A/B probe on lean_cic/spiral_proves; 11:3x A/B (tmp/hopac-speed/ab-threadlocal.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, runs 2-3): the 4 hot-path JP slots AsyncLocal -> ThreadLocal gave identical outputs on 4 samples but lean_cic 6.15/6.50 -> 6.15/6.20 s, spiral_proves 6.10/6.15 -> 5.95/6.10 s, brzozowski and hello within noise (A alone varies 5% between rounds): no proven gain, reverted; the installBridge 7.4% is not the AsyncLocal write cost; next: allocation profile (gc-verbose) of term on lean_cic; 11:4x runtime knobs on the unchanged binary (ab-runtime*.ps1, 2 interleaved rounds, baseline noise about 0.3 s): the shipped config (server GC, concurrent, TieredPGO off) is already best: gen0 64 MB +10%, gen0 8 MB within noise, TieredCompilation off +15%, workstation GC +20-25% on lean_cic/spiral_proves (the hopac core allocates from several threads); GC tuning is ruled out; remaining levers: startup JIT (ReadyToRun, hello is 1.6 s of mostly startup) and algorithmic work in term; 11:4x gen0: test.ps1 sets DOTNET_GCgen0size=256 MB for every run; fresh-process probes (ab-gen0.ps1) show it 4-13% slower than the runtime default on lean_cic/spiral_proves in BOTH cores; but an A-B-A single-flight suite (aba-gen0-suite.ps1, warm workers, 236 C rows) shows no difference (summed 62.5 / 67.6 default / 67.4 s); test.ps1 left unchanged: switching it needs an A-B-A hopac -Suite all (fresh process per job) showing the gain; ReadyToRun is already applied by build.ps1 since 2026-10-05; 12:3x hopac harness default now warm-recycle with deferral (HOPAC-WARM-SERVER): suite 2.35x faster at identical rows; 2026-10-12: hermetic BackendSwitch validation made the hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validating every arm, per-build type cache); candidate: type-check other arms without evaluating their join points; ladders 2026-10-12: hopac 67 min vs single-flight 27-35 min on -Suite all -Native; user decision 2026-10-12 19:05: hopac remains the intended default once it matches single-flight (raised to P0 with HOPAC-WATER-OIL); user decision 2026-10-12 19:18: water-oil first, then speed: every speed fix after unification is written once; 06:22 suite wall times tonight (same machine, varying load from parallel lanes): single-flight 19-23 min per -Suite all -Native; hopac 64, 41, 47, 48 and 29 min (pack-0005, least contended: hopac 29 vs single-flight 23); user decision 2026-10-13 07:08: hopac becomes the default core when its -Suite all -Native wall time is within 5% of single-flight on an idle machine with identical results (tonight's least-contended run: hopac 29 min vs single-flight 23 min); 2026-10-13 16:07 main, early estimate from today's suites (paired ok rows, compile_ms; the two runs had different machine load, so an idle measurement follows tonight): contracts hopac/sf sum ratio 15.2 (median 8.5, p90 50.6), examples 5.1 (median 7.1, p90 19.3), frontier 2.4, mega 1.8; worst samples are the mega roots on native backends (runtime_bench sf 7.8 s vs hopac 249 s, indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 8.6 vs 173 s); the user's switch rule (hopac default at equal suite time, within 5% idle) is far from met: hopac stays the candidate lane; 2026-10-13 18:36 main, interleaved A/B measurement (hopac_speed_ab.ps1 in main's job tmp: per sample, single-flight and hopac Release alternate in fresh --batch processes, 2 rounds, best of each, C backend; background load hits both modes alike): 14 frontier+mega samples sum sf 56.3 s vs hopac 149.0 s = 2.65x, median 1.83x; fixed startup overhead ~1.4 s (frontier_hello 2.5 vs 3.9 s); the gap grows with evaluation size: mega_brzozowski_derivatives/runtime_bench 5.3 vs 36.6 s (6.9x), mega_zeta 4.8x, omniledger 3.4x; next target: profile runtime_bench on hopac (probe.ps1 -Profile) for the evaluation hot path, and the startup cost separately; switch rule (equal suite time within 5%) far from met; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, sampled profile (probe.ps1 runtime_bench -Mode hopac -Backend C -Profile 30; logs in spiral-bin/probes/mega_brzozowski_derivatives__runtime_bench): innermost compiler frames ~37% evaluation (term 12.0, body.Invoke 23.1, peval 1.7) vs ~30% join-point coordination bookkeeping (jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 9.1, jpTryReserveDeclaredBodyLane 4.3, jpNoteDeclaredResumeEvidence 3.2, jpSetCurrentDeclaredSafeContinuationAtDepth 3.1, jpMaybePruneDeclaredResumeSnapshots 3.1, jpPersistDeclaredBodyCheckpoint 2.4, observer lane 1.5, continuation ordinal 1.3, retire snapshot 1.2) and ~8% diagnostics/sampling (DiagJson runner+loop 6.2, JpNativeThreadCpuClock 1.9; probe.ps1 sets SPIRAL_DIAG_QUIET: yes); first target: the declared-body resume-evidence/checkpoint machinery (race-34 replay store) on the hot path - make it lazy or batch it, measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1; 2026-10-13 18:38 main, caveat on the profile above: the shares are thread-stack samples, not CPU time; DiagJson.runner/loop is the progress HUD ticker, which in quiet mode only sleeps in 25 ms slices, so its 6.2% is a sleeping thread; re-run the breakdown on the evaluator thread only (python scripts/profile-thread.py <run.speedscope.json> [thread]) before optimizing the join-point bookkeeping; 2026-10-13 18:39 main, evaluator-thread CPU profile (profile-thread.py run1.speedscope.json, busiest thread 29.1 s busy, inclusive): jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation 22.4-23.3%, buildFreshApplyState 17.8%, jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation 7.6% (the rest is evaluation: term/apply/if); ~half of hopac's evaluator CPU is per-step replay/resume bookkeeping single-flight does not have; next: make jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation and the let-continuation store lazy (record only when a suspension can actually resume) and reuse apply state instead of buildFreshApplyState per apply, then re-measure with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x, runtime_bench 6.9x); 2026-10-13 20:02 main, next-session plan from reading jpRecordCurrentWorkOperation (spiral_compiler.fs ~145856, 23% of hopac evaluator CPU, 2 callers): per evaluator step it interns shapeText (OperationalReasonIdOps.create, a string hash per step) although only 1 in 1024 steps uses the counted shape, does a ConcurrentDictionary GetOrAdd for the job profile, takes a lock to write five last-seen fields, and polls DiagJson.terminalFailureExitRequestedNow; behaviour-neutral fixes: keep the raw shape text and intern only on sampled/read paths, cache the job profile in the owner's AsyncLocal slot instead of GetOrAdd per step, replace the per-step lock with owner-local fields published at sample points; then buildFreshApplyState (17.8%) and jpStoreDeclaredLetContinuation (7.6%); measure each with hopac_speed_ab.ps1 (baseline 2.65x overall, runtime_bench 6.9x) and keep hopac's full suite at 0 behaviour differences; 2026-10-13 22:47 naming: the core this item calls hopac is now gpt5parallel (user decision; SPIRAL_CORE_GPT5PARALLEL, -Mode gpt5parallel, lanes/gpt5parallel); the id keeps HOPAC until eoie can rename ids (EOIE-AGILE-RENAME-ID); the planned rewrite is HOPACSOTA; scope now: the measured baseline (2.65x overall, 6.9x runtime_bench, ~half of evaluator CPU in replay bookkeeping) is hopacsota's target to beat, rather than a patch list for gpt5parallel; closed 2026-10-13 23:34 as superseded by hopacsota (user decision): goal inherited by HOPACSOTA-P3 (speed) and Phase 1/2 speed gates", Epic, P0, D7, 100u32, Done, "HOPAC-WATER-OIL", "compiler,hopac,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_hopac_speed () = Planning ("HOPAC-SPEED", Minutes 2400u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Pair)
inl portable_primitives () = Task ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", "Replace C-only macros with portable ops: StringConcat, Floor/Ceil/Round/Atan2, string search/case, a char model, Zig/Lean/Bend arms in lib backend switches; 2026-10-08 13:1x lib/spiral math (edited in math.livemd via mix spiral.edit, math.spi re-exported and compared): floor/ceil/atan2/round gain C (__builtin_* with _Generic for f32/f64), Cpp (<cmath>), Delphi (Int/Frac/Ord, ArcTan2) and TypeScript arms; Lua and Gleam round fixed (gave 2 for 2.7 and 4 for 3.2; now the shared round_half_to_even); lib backend.spi dispatches C and Delphi (before: no lib function could reach a C arm); new sample portable_math_rounding: 10 backends, 9 native runs exit 0, all agree with the C oracle; 13:0x blessed the lib-using samples (30 rows; review and bless runs identical): portable_math_rounding new on 10 backends; format_any x2 C/Delphi now fail at the C/Pascal compiler with backend_switch_has_no_<backend>_arm_in_lib_spiral (BackendSwitch evaluates every arm, so a lib fallback cannot raise a Spiral error for one backend only, same as the Zig/Lean fallbacks); 7 F#/Rust/Zig rows changed only in variable numbering (proved by normalizing v<n>); next: console write_line/write for C and Delphi via the portable !!!!Printf with a typecase, which needs one agreed bool/float text across backends (F# True vs Rust true); 17:1x lib console (console.livemd via spiral.edit, console.spi re-exported): write_line/write gain C arms (printf via real typecase helpers write_line_c/write_c: string %s, i32, i64, u32, u64; floats and bools stay out: no shortest round-trip float text in C, F#/Python print True) and Delphi arms (Writeln/Write); TypeScript write_line moved from console.log to process.stdout.write(String(x)+newline): the harness runs TS in a Worker where console.log and process.stdout.write reorder, and console.log printed i64 BigInt as -7n; new sample portable_console_lines: 8 native backends, stdout identical to C; lib samples re-reviewed (zig_lib_console changed only in numbering); 19:5x lib convert (convert.livemd): convert_i32_base gains C (strtol) and Delphi (FPC radix prefixes ##36/##37/##38, base 2/8/10/16) arms; convert_i32 gains C/Delphi/TypeScript typed dispatch (string -> base 10, float -> round half even via nearbyint/Round, int -> cast); try_convert from string gains C/Delphi/TypeScript/Lua arms through parsed_integer (strict whole-text validation, never-throwing value expression, target range check via integer_fits) and Gleam trims like Rust; Lua previously returned the unparsed string, TypeScript had no convert_i32; lib console Gleam write_line/write print ints via int.to_string (io.println took only String); new sample portable_convert_parse: 7 native backends print 255/11/-42/123/none/none/7 like C; portable_console_lines now has a Gleam row; 19:1x triaged what is left: the lib backend_switch fallback in lib/spiral/backend.spi already carries Zig and Lean arms (they only compileError or name the missing arm for non-unit types) and math (5/5/5), console (3/3/3) and optionm (5/5/5) are fully covered, but the switchable backends run_target_args' and the backend union itself know only Gleam, Lua, Fsharp, Cuda, Cpp, Rust and TypeScript, so a Zig or Lean arm there needs a design call on extending a union that every consumer matches exhaustively; the lib files with not one zig or lean arm are date_time (28 switches), listm (10), resultm (10), seq (9), trace (9), runtime (4), async (3), crypto (3), env (3) and the single-switch common, guid, platform, reflection and testing, while base (23 switches, 5/5), am (13, 3/3) and sm (27, 12/11) are partly covered; nothing is blocked on this today because the harness matrix runs only the C and Gleam backends (the 19:0x restore rung: 610 rows = 483 C + 127 Gleam, no Zig or Lean row exists to fail) and a new arm cannot be validated without a sample, which tonight's scope forbids; StringConcat, string search and case ops and a char model remain compiler-op additions that need every backend and both cores", Feature, P1, D6, 60u32, Active, "", "compiler,lib,portability", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_portable_primitives () = Planning ("PORTABLE-PRIMITIVES", Minutes 720u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl zig_float_format () = Task ("ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT", "Zig build time: std.fmt float printing adds ~0.4 s per program; a lean shortest-round-trip formatter matching the C oracle; 2026-10-10 measured on zig 0.17: the float arm is compiled only when a float is printed (an i32 print and a program that prints nothing both cold-build in about 9s; f32 and f64 cold-build in about 10-11s; a warm f64 after an i32 cache is about 2s). Printf still rejects a float argument, so no current program pays that cost. The printer is still unwritten.; 2026-10-13 18:05 Kino lane: Zig floats now print canonically (shortest round-trip via std.fmt {d}, .0 for integral values, NaN/Infinity/-Infinity) through sm'.show/format, asserted in sm' show_floats on zig; the build-time part (a lean formatter instead of std.fmt, ~0.4 s per program) is still open", TaskKind, P2, D5, 50u32, Active, "", "backend,zig,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_zig_float_format () = Planning ("ZIG-FLOAT-FORMAT", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl gleam_parity () = Task ("GLEAM-PARITY", "Gleam: closures inside recursive unions, BEAM has no inf/NaN; 1 DISAGREE left is Known; done 2026-10-11: native_closure_union_rec runs exit 70 agreeing with C (TyConv keys on the source primitive) and native_float_infinity joins the Gleam list as the Known row (BEAM floats are never infinite or NaN: a 0x7FF0000000000000 float bit-pattern match is nomatch, binary_to_term raises badarg), ladders single-flight-20261011-174150 / hopac-20261011-174720 / restore single-flight-20261011-175313 each ran C,Gleam examples with native: 610 jobs, oracle agree 126, known 1, DISAGREE 0, hopac missing 0, script exit 0, restore 0 of 610 rows differ", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "backend,gleam", "current", "current")
inl clean_generated_output () = Task ("CLEAN-GENERATED-OUTPUT", "Zero toolchain warnings and no comments in generated code across backends (Lean partial-def warnings suppressed by linters); 2026-10-08 13:3x measured C: the harness builds C with -w (all warnings suppressed); gcc -Wall -Wextra -fsyntax-only over the 208 committed main.c: 126 files warn (211 -Wunused-parameter, 180 -Wunused-but-set-variable); the 31 hard errors are expected (27 C-ABI samples linked with harness shims, 2 format_any lib arms deferred to the C compiler, 2 POSIX poll.h cube samples); fixes ((void)param for unused parameters, no set-but-unused temporaries) are in CodegenC = upstream section, so they go with COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION; then drop -w from the harness so warnings fail the native tier; 13:4x C -Wunused-parameter fixed: empty refcount bodies (ArrayDecrefBody, ClosureDecrefBody, heap DecrefBody) emit (void)x; (114 C outputs changed only by that line and one blank line; native unchanged); left: 180 -Wunused-but-set-variable (match results stored and never read: dead stores from peval, needs dead-store elimination), the // case comments on C switch labels (go with READABLE-GENERATED-NAMES); 18:4x fresh gcc -Wall -Wextra -fsyntax-only sweep over all 462 committed main.c: 382 clean, 31 hard errors (15 abi_external, 10 dynamic_array_growth/resize, the 2 format_any lib arms, the 2 POSIX cube samples, 2 native_managed_array_scc), 213 warnings in 49 files = 204 -Wunused-but-set-variable (was 180, new samples landed), 8 -Wunused-parameter, 1 -Wtautological-compare; two corrections to the record: the // case comments on C switch labels are gone (0 comment lines in the 462 main.c, and 0 across main.rs 402, main.pas 391, main.fsx 516, main.kt 246, main.swift 246, main.lean 135, main.wat 147, main.ts 117, main.lua 135, main.gleam 127, main.bend 120; the only comments left anywhere are the 11 main.zig build-header lines), and -Wunused-parameter is not fully fixed: the 8 remaining are closure body runtime parameters that the body never reads (closure_array v1, closure_depth v2, closure_heap v1, closure_mut v1, closure_return_heap v2, closure_return_text v2, method_depth v1, mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned x), unlike the refcount bodies that already emit (void)x; the one -Wtautological-compare is ebpf_conv compiling its own m <= m source comparison (main.spi line 23), not a codegen defect; 19:4x fixed the closure-body half of the leftover: the C closure printer now void-casts exactly the domain arguments the body never reads (it collects the body's free variables once through the existing refc_used_vars walk and emits (void)vN; only for arguments absent from it), a three-line change in the shared CodegenC section that both cores build; the full-suite run single-flight-20261011-190603 regenerated the outputs with 7 files each gaining one (void)vN; line (closure_array v1, closure_depth v2, closure_heap v1, closure_mut v1, closure_return_heap v2, closure_return_text v2, method_depth v1) at 3774 jobs exit 0, native 2749 ran with 13 build-fail, oracle agree 2284, DISAGREE 0 and missing|regressed 0; re-sweeping the same 462 main.c gives 389 clean, 42 warning files, 206 warnings = 204 -Wunused-but-set-variable, the ebpf_conv tautological compare and 1 -Wunused-parameter, and that last one is the stale mega_brzozowski_derivatives/bench_interned output that predates the 13:4x (void)x; fix (425 of 426 empty decref bodies carry it), and it did not clear in tonight's three full-suite runs: that sample is only in the suite as a Fsharp contracts job, its main.fsx refreshed at 20:47 with the same residual b00257ff342f6cce in rung 1 and in the restore, while its main.c still sits at 10-06 16:36, so no suite run regenerates its C file and clearing it needs a targeted C regeneration of that sample; 5 mega samples also drifted in that run (Rust impl Default blocks and the (v5)->refc++ parenthesization of earlier changes), pre-existing and benign; codegen proof ladder 