inl backend_switch_hermetic () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-HERMETIC", "BackendSwitch evaluation, state of the art for both cores: every non-current arm is evaluated in key order on every backend for its type only (compile-time state identical across backends, a type mismatch is an error everywhere, like Kotlin expect/actual), hermetically (throwaway seq, its own variable counter from 1<<<30 so no tag reaches the residual: single-flight had numbered around the discarded arms, the cause of the BackendSwitch cross-core residual class), once per (backend, arm closure) per build (type cache; hopac gains one, cleared with the JP dicts). Hopac validates again (backend_switch_validate_all on; it skipped other arms since 2026-10-02 for speed, unsound). 2026-10-12: edits in spiral_compiler.fs, unbuilt; proof ladder single-flight -> hopac -> restore pending; proven and blessed 2026-10-12 05:07: cross-core residual differences 83 -> 0, behaviour differences 0, hopac missing 0, restore 0 of 3,768; hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validation cost ~40% under full load: next, evaluate type-check-only join points inline); 2026-10-12 closed: blessed twice (dev40 05:07, dev41 08:36), cross-core 0 differences; the ~40% hopac validation cost moves to HOPAC-SPEED", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,hopac,parity", "current", "current")
inl c_format_any () = Task ("C-FORMAT-ANY", "C arm for lib sm_real format_debug_real (format of any value): frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any C rows build-fail on backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral, so Rust has no oracle there, and sm format test has no C expectation. Structure can reuse the portable serialize_union_rust/record_fold/tuple arms (as Zig/Lean do); the C-specific gap is primitive-to-string: lib globals precede the C prelude that defines StringLit, so a lib helper cannot build a String; needs a compiler-emitted helper or a portable Spiral digit loop; 2026-10-12 done in lib (sm.livemd cells 46/58/87, exported sm_real.spir): append_real C via StaticStringConcat, format_debug_real C arm (snprintf into a static buffer, String built at the call site with StringLit; shortest round-trip floats, quoted chars; unions/tuples/records/nominals/arrays via the portable arms), format_real C arms (empty, prim p with %+.6f/NaN/Infinity, append). frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any C now build and exit 0 (were build-fail); a probe of union/record/tuple/f32 formatting prints byte-identical text in C and Rust; 2026-10-12 compiler lane: C rows frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any build, exit 0, Rust agrees; Delphi still fails (backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral): needs a Delphi arm in lib/spiral BackendSwitch; 07:47 Delphi done (lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir, uncommitted): Delphi arms for append_real (s + x), format_debug_real (bool BoolToStr true/false, char, floats FloatToStr, tuples/records/unions/nominals through the portable arms, ints IntToStr) and format_real's three switches (empty, per-value: floats signed fixed 6 decimals, chars, strings; append); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any Delphi now build with fpc and exit 0 (were build-fail); oracle rows flip at the next pack's bless", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,c,format", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_format_any () = Planning ("C-FORMAT-ANY", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl self_checking_samples () = Task ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", "Samples and tests as self-checking source (user design session 2026-10-12, nothing fixed yet; dogfood before committing): no prose, code only; expectations live inline in the source of truth, expect-test style (ppx_expect, Rust expect-test/insta, Zig test blocks): a promote run rewrites the expected blocks in place, git diff is the review, a clean run leaves the file untouched, dev mode can persist cell results and clear them after a successful run; the oracle backend is configurable per file or cell (C default) and other backends are checked against it, replacing hand-written per-backend copies and the uncommittable cache EXPECTED.tsv; end state: .spi itself is the notebook (compiler-understood test blocks with inline expectations, Zig/Rust #[test]-like), .livemd becomes a view, so edits are plain file edits; negative samples stay as test blocks whose expectation is a diagnostic (rustc UI tests, clang expected-error); multi-file packages must work (package-manager litmus): measure the dice symlink cost on Windows, then compare compiler-resolved path dependencies (Cargo path/workspaces) with junctions. Phases: 1 benchmark the same sample family through the harness and through Kino (warm workers, parallel cells: KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE); 2 measure dice symlink package resolution on Windows; 3 dogfood one family (ebpf_* 27 samples) as an inline-expectation file with promote mode, keeping harness rows byte-identical; 4 decide; phase 2 first look 2026-10-12: dice reaches lib through two chained symlinks (dice/deps/polyglot -> polyglot, polyglot/deps/spiral -> spiral); raw I/O over the 315 lib .spi/.spir files through the chain is 1.4-2.5x slower warm (hundreds of ms at worst, not the main cost); the compiler normalizes package paths with Path.GetFullPath, which collapses .. but does not resolve links, so a package identity is the path spelling: the same lib reached as dice\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral\\lib and as spiral\\lib is two packages (parsed, cached and watched twice), the likelier cost and a blocker for one canonical identity per package in a package manager; next: time a dice compile via the link path vs the direct path, then try canonicalizing package roots (FinalPath/ResolveLinkTarget) in the compiler; test block prototype 2026-10-12: survey of the 39 lib notebooks: 1,904 cells, 364 test cells (44 static, 12 in .spir real code, 11 print_code), targets fsharp 169 / cuda 126 / rust 107 / gleam 93 / typescript 93 / lua 34 / c 1, per-target options (rust -d crates, gleam -t javascript -d), all timeouts default; test cells hold top-level-only statements (open, nominal, #r), so a test is a scoped group of top-level statements, not a function: syntax `///> target opts` lines then `test name [static] [print_code] =` with an indented body; semantics = file prefix up to the block + the body (Kino notebook-prefix semantics, Spiral is top-down so position preserves scope); compiler: blockParsingParse returns Error [] for a block starting with `test` (both cores; the same no-statement result as comment-only blocks), unbuilt; converter livemd -> spi/spir with test blocks reproduces the current exports byte for byte in 38 of 40 files (benchmark and lustre carry stale doc text from cell outputs); spi -> livemd adapter round-trips all 37 notebooks with every test source preserved (order of .spir tests and one directive line moved); next: run original vs regenerated notebooks through Kino and compare per-test results, then the build with the parser change; dice package identity fix (2026-10-12, unbuilt): canonical_directory in the shared SpiProj schema resolves every path component with Directory.ResolveLinkTarget (symlinks and junctions), used for packageDir/moduleDir combines and the default package dir, so a package reached through deps/polyglot/deps/spiral and through spiral/ is one package; no harness path crosses a link (checked), so sample outputs must stay byte-identical; to measure: a dice compile through the link path before and after, and dice/alphabet packageDir pointing straight at the sibling ../spiral/lib (CI and local init already create that layout); dice measured 2026-10-12 (cold compile of lib/dice.spi to Rust, one process each): old compiler through the symlinks 19.3 s, new compiler (canonical package dirs + tonight batch) through the symlinks 17.4 s, new compiler with packageDir ../spiral/lib (no links) 18.0 s; the new compiler output is byte-identical through links and without them (one package identity), so dice can drop the links; a single cold compile shows no real symlink cost, so the slowdown the user saw likely sits elsewhere (cargo/rustc over symlinked dirs, Kino watching, repeated warm compiles): measure those next. Test blocks: with the new parser all 364 test blocks embedded in 35 lib files compile to byte-identical C (pristine-copy check; an earlier failure came from stale files in a reused copy, not from test blocks); 2026-10-12 lib/spiral migrated (unstaged): the 39 lib notebooks are now their .spi (370 test blocks; sm' keeps its real-mode code and tests in sm'_real.spir); the 39 .livemd are deleted; Kino document domain (apps/kino/spi/document.spi, Spiral) gained to_spi_tested/to_spir_tested (test cells -> test blocks named by heading) and from_spi/parse_spi (blocks -> test cells), byte-identical to the validated prototype on all 37 notebooks (644 cells); run_notebook.ps1 --path x.spi runs a source file as a notebook and never writes over it; lib/spiral/build.ps1 and near/wallet/build.ps1 run .spi and write <nb>.spi.ipynb (README links -> .spi.html); evidence: per-test A/B original livemd vs round trip 353 tests in 31 notebooks same outcome, .spi run directly math/seq/sm' 79 tests same, the 7 lib-dependent harness samples 53 rows identical to the blessed repro, Kino mix test 140/0; open: the full lib build from .spi (running 09:30), then folder samples -> test blocks; 10:10 user committed the lib migration (dc7b955c 09:51); all 39 lib notebooks run from .spi with exit 0 (Kino run_notebook, 2 at a time), 268 tests in 29 notebooks have the same outcome as the original livemd runs (physics/parsing/rust outputs vanished before comparison, both matched in the A/B); .spi -> .livemd kept for a cell UI: from_spi now restores /// doc runs as markdown cells and adds a #### name (test) heading only when the name would not re-derive; run_notebook --livemd-path writes the Livebook view, --with-tests exports back; 39/39 lib notebooks .spi -> .livemd -> .spi byte-identical, 26/39 livemd byte-identical to the deleted originals, 3,503 of 3,531 old cells verbatim (sm' real cells move ahead of the rest); Kino mix test covers both properties; UI direction: Zed (LSP + runnables, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES) as the editor, Livebook via --livemd-path for rich outputs; split 2026-10-12 into SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS, TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, KINO-RUN-ONE-TEST, NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS, SPIR-INTERLEAVED-LIVEMD, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES; children also TEST-RUN-REPORT, TEST-IDENTITY-I18N, SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY (from the 2026-10-12 external review of dc7b955c)", Epic, P1, D7, 55u32, Active, "", "samples,kino,compiler,dogfood,packages", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_self_checking_samples () = Planning ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl test_signal_metrics () = Task ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", "Test signal and cost metrics drive sample cleanup (2026-10-12, mined from 926 single-flight + 710 hopac runs since 09-26, 362,684 rows): silent rows (no text or outcome change in 20+ runs) are only 3.5% of median cost, so pruning by silence alone saves little; Kotlin is 41% of the total cost (about 92 min native for 253 rows, ~22 s each, JVM kotlinc startup) with 13 rows that ever flipped: batch all Kotlin samples into one kotlinc run (one package per sample) and run each with java -cp; F# is 21% (compile only, mega_omniledger 180 s); the nested-combination families heap_/closure_/union_/array_/case_/method_/mut_ hold ~1,600 rows and about half the cost with at most 50 ever-flipped rows (case_: 323 rows, 0 flips): compact them into composite programs that cover many combinations per compile, keeping every ever-flipped row standalone; highest-signal families: native_ 391 flipped rows, dynamic_ 169, managed_ 108, ts_ 76, frontier_ 52; text-change co-change twins (1,965) are driven by global renames, so redundancy must be measured on outcome flips; next: Kotlin batching in test.ps1, a fast tier (all ever-flipped rows + one per family) for the edit loop, full matrix for bless; Kotlin done 2026-10-12: tests/native-shims/kotlin-server/KotlinServer.java compiles every Kotlin row of a run in one warm JVM (K2JVMCompiler in process, -no-stdlib against kotlin-stdlib.jar), a pre-pass in test.ps1 before the native lanes, lanes only run java -cp; Kotlin row median 285 ms vs ~22 s (single-flight-20261012-050734, all 246 rows agree, bless + reproduction 0 differences); a full single-flight suite 50 -> 26 min; next: same treatment for Swift (swiftc ~3 s/row), compaction of the low-signal families, the fast tier; 2026-10-12 12:00 recomputed over all single-flight runs (6,079 rows, 4,011 with >=5 runs, summed row time 8,162 s): signal (output changes + 2x status flips) concentrates in native_ (3,546), dynamic_ (1,218), managed_ (1,084), frontier_ (779), ts_ (631); silent cost concentrates in array_ (200 of 270 rows silent, 1,106 s), heap_ (205/276, 978 s), union_ (116/192), method_ (91/126), mut_ (65/90); negative_/contract_ rows are silent by design (stable rejections); coverage-first fast tiers (every backend and family, then signal per second): 300 s = 734 rows / 3.7% of the time / 51% of historical signal, 600 s = 1,098 / 7.4% / 73%, 1,200 s = 1,657 / 14.7% / 88%; lists in the job scratch (metrics/fast-tier-*.tsv, compaction.py); next: a harness fast suite from the 600 s list (harness.psd1, compiler lane) and folding the silent combinatorial families into fewer test blocks (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS); caveat: signal = changed during development, a proxy, not proof a row catches bugs", Feature, P1, D4, 70u32, Active, "", "harness,metrics,samples,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_signal_metrics () = Planning ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl fsharp_utf8_strings () = Task ("FSHARP-UTF8-STRINGS", "F# string semantics are .NET UTF-16 while Spiral strings index and measure UTF-8 bytes (C, Kotlin spiralStringIndex, Swift utf8, Wasm, Lean byte positions): 2026-10-12 once F# gained StaticStringConcat, samples/string_utf8 compiles and its .fsx exits 1 under dotnet fsi where C exits 0 (string_eq exits 0 like C); the harness never runs F# natively (test.ps1 excludes Fsharp from native jobs), so F# semantic gaps stay hidden: give F# UTF-8 byte semantics for length/index/slice (Encoding.UTF8 helpers like the Kotlin backend) and add an F# native tier through dotnet fsi or a compiled exe so F# rows are checked against the C oracle", TaskKind, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "fsharp,backend,strings,harness", "current", "current")
