inl tibia_fidelity_step_timing () = Task ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-STEP-TIMING", "Step duration fidelity (speed, ground speed, diagonal penalty) is still unmeasured: a client-side probe timing player:isWalking() around g_game.walk reported ~10 ms per step with no position change, because OTClient pre-walks locally and the GOD character runs at GM speed 1500 (tibia-local/probe-timing.lua, 2026-10-12); measure with a normal-speed character by timing server-confirmed position updates (onCreaturePositionChange / onWalk events) or from the server side, then replace the simulator's one-tile-per-tick movement with the measured step durations; 2026-10-13 04:24 first server-side measurement (tibia-local/probe-steps-body.lua via run-policy-scenario.ps1 -ProbeBody probe-steps-body.lua, monster onPositionChange timestamps): troll straight steps median 1009 ms (n=34, min 999) while idle-wandering near 1932,1349,7; the model (creatures.spi step_ms, Canary formula on grass ground speed 150) predicts 950 ms, so the local ground is slower (about 155-160) or idle walking adds a cost; not yet measured: chase steps (x2 WALK_TARGET_NEARBY_EXTRA_COST; these trolls did not chase), diagonal x3, fleeing, player steps; next: read the tile's ground speed from the map/items.xml, measure a chase; 2026-10-13 11:56 new lead from the live cyclops arena: the simulator's clear mode (hunting_pack.clear_run, pack_sim cyclops_clear_predictions_for_the_live_arena) predicts rules 88.7 s / 15.6 potions and kiting-at-3 92.7 s / 15.2 per clear (20 seeds); live (2 runs each, forge off) shows rules about 104 s / 15 and kiting about 79 s / 12.5; potions match for rules, but live kiting gains where the simulator predicts none; suspect creature re-approach after a retreat (the simulator steps approaching foes every step_ms x 2) and the live probe's strike-readiness guess; measure cyclops chase steps with probe-steps-body; 2026-10-13 12:05 Canary walking rules read from source (creature.cpp getStepDuration, monster.cpp getDistanceStep): diagonal steps cost x3 for every creature (players too); straight steps cost x2 once a monster has been adjacent to its target (a counter that only rises while adjacent and is never decremented by a melee monster, because getDistanceStep returns early beyond the target distance); before first contact x1; the simulator used x2 for every approach, no diagonal cost for anyone; hunting_pack now uses geometry.step_cost (diagonal_step_factor 3) and a foe met_target flag with chase_step_factor 2 for foes and the player; probe-steps-body has a chase mode (TIBIA_STEPS_CHASE=1) to confirm live; 2026-10-13 12:36 first chase-mode run (arena-logs/cyclops-steps-chase-*.log): cyclops straight steps before contact 701-713 ms (x1 on a ~650 ms base after the 50 ms server-beat rounding, as the new model says; the old model used x2), but the knight never walked away, so post-contact and diagonal steps are still unmeasured; the steps probe logs no server text, so the cause is unknown (next: log walk results and texts); 2026-10-13 18:25 chase steps measured live (probe-steps-body chase mode, one cyclops): after contact 701-715 ms (median 712, n=7), the same as before contact (median 712, n=4), so Canary's target-nearby x2 does not apply to a melee chase in practice; the cyclops base step is ~700-715 ms on the arena ground vs 650 by the grass formula (+8%, the troll measurement showed +6%); the simulator now uses chase_step_factor 1 and terrain.ground_step_percent 108 on ArenaSpot for every step; diagonal x3 still unmeasured (no diagonal chase step occurred)", TaskKind, P3, D3, 70u32, Active, "TIBIA-OFFSCREEN-PROBES", "tibia,oracle", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_fidelity_step_timing () = Planning ("TIBIA-FIDELITY-STEP-TIMING", Minutes 180u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl tibia_evolved_policy () = Task ("TIBIA-EVOLVED-POLICY", "First learning loop, apps/tibia/hunt_sim.spi 2026-10-12: a (1,lambda)-ES (evolve_policy: 4 generations x 6 offspring, spread 64 halving, keyed RNG, no arrays) tunes Tuned (heal_below, strike_above) against the Cyclops on training seeds 1..6, scored kills - 50 * deaths; on held-out seeds 21..30 it finds heal 124 / strike 23 with score 8, equal to the hand-tuned baseline ScriptedHealingBelow 120 (8) and far above the random control (-448), Rust and C agree, search plus evaluation runs in ~100 ms; per the arena rule a tie is not a promotion: next, richer policy features (creature health, distance, cooldown state), more offspring/generations, and the graph-code network once arrays exist; user decision 2026-10-12 19:18: after the GPU/test blockers, the learning agent comes first (policy/learning loop on the simulator), before the headless client and fidelity work; 2026-10-13 03:53 under the combat model the (1,lambda)-ES finds the same heal 124 / strike 23 and scores 14 on held-out seeds vs the tuned baseline's 16 (control -443): it now trails, test renamed evolved_policy_trails_tuned_baseline_and_beats_random_control_on_held_out_seeds; 2026-10-13 06:14 superseded by TIBIA-FAIR-CHASING-BASELINE and TIBIA-POLICY-FEATURES: the ES on 20 training seeds ties a cavebot tuned on the same seeds (90 vs 90 held-out)", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "TIBIA-SCENARIO-HARDNESS", "tibia,arena,ml", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_tibia_evolved_policy () = Planning ("TIBIA-EVOLVED-POLICY", Minutes 600u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl wgsl_payload_unions () = Task ("WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", "WGSL step 2 (user 2026-10-12: finish the WGSL backend first so GPU work does not clog): unions with payloads as a u32 tag plus a flattened payload struct (one field group per case, or a shared max-width slot layout), union unbox as a switch on the tag; unblocks apps/tibia vitality typestate and spell groups (hunt, hunt_sim, vitality GPU tests); verify every union sample in the harness on WGSL against the C oracle via spiral-wgsl; 19:52 implemented and verified on the GPU (uncommitted, single-flight Debug 19:50, 0 errors): a union with payloads becomes struct SpiralUnionN { tag: u32, c<case>f<field>: T, ... } (every case's flattened fields; zero-value T() for the others), TyUnionBox builds it with the case's values, TyUnionUnbox tests v.tag per arm (any number of scrutinees) and binds the case's fields; recursive unions raise 'recursive unions (WGSL has no pointers)'; probe tmp/wgsl/payload_union (Circle : i32 | Rect : i32 * i32 | Empty, match + union equality): 2,973-byte shader, spiral-wgsl exit 124, C exit 124; nullary probe still 170/170; remaining: harness sample rows and a hopac build; 20:03: nominal-typed unions (a union passed to a let method arrives as YNominal) now resolve through env.nominal_apply; samples/wgsl_nullary_union (170), samples/wgsl_payload_union (124), gleam_match_single_case (49) and gleam_match_narrowed (61) compile to WGSL and spiral-wgsl returns the C exit for each (single-flight Debug 20:01); all four added to the Wgsl list in tests/harness.psd1; the unsupported-type error now names the Ty constructor; next: a suite run so the four Wgsl rows enter the oracle, and a hopac build", TaskKind, P0, D5, 100u32, Done, "WGSL-NULLARY-UNIONS", "wgsl,compiler,tibia", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_wgsl_payload_unions () = Planning ("WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", Minutes 360u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl wgsl_storage_arrays () = Task ("WGSL-STORAGE-ARRAYS", "WGSL step 3: arrays as storage buffers (fixed-size first, then runtime-sized with bindings), so array samples and the ML/arena code (TIBIA-AGENT, TIBIA-2048-ANCHOR) can run on the GPU; harness rows against the C oracle via spiral-wgsl; 20:13 step 1 implemented (uncommitted, single-flight Debug 20:11, 0 errors): arrays whose element is one scalar (i32/u32/f32/bool) live in a module-private heap var<private> spiral_heap: array<u32, 16384> with a bump allocator spiral_array_create (length word, then elements bitcast to u32, never freed; emitted only when an array is created); an array value is its u32 offset; ArrayIndex/ArrayIndexSet/ArrayLength map to heap slots; samples/wgsl_array_scalar (i32 + bool arrays, runtime index, length) spiral-wgsl 135 = C 135 (the first draft read an unset bool slot: C does not zero-fill new arrays, so the sample sets every slot); fixed_array_scalar / runtime_index / runtime_update also run on WGSL and agree with C (exit 0); all four on the Wgsl list; next: multi-word elements (unions, tuples: array_union fails with 'array elements of type SpiralUnion0'), a storage-buffer heap bound by the runner for large data, and bounds checks (naga clamps out-of-range indices today); 20:18 step 2: multi-word elements: an element type flattens to words (a payload union = tag + every case's fields, nested unions recursively, nominals resolved), reads rebuild the struct from its words, writes store each field, spiral_array_create takes a stride; single-flight Debug 20:16 (0 errors): all 9 union/array samples on WGSL match C (wgsl_array_scalar 135, fixed_array_* 0, array_union 0, wgsl_payload_union 124, wgsl_nullary_union 170, gleam_match_narrowed 61, gleam_match_single_case 49); array_union added to the Wgsl list (10 new Wgsl rows tonight); still open: elements made of several values (tuples), a storage-buffer heap for large data, bounds checks; 21:25 step 3, storage buffers: the spiral-wgsl runner (tests/native-shims/wgsl-run/src/main.rs, uncommitted; rebuilt into the toolchains/wgsl-run target dir, previous binary kept at tmp/wgsl/spiral-wgsl.before-heap.exe) binds a zero-initialized heap buffer at binding 1 (SPIRAL_WGSL_HEAP_MB, default 64, capped at the device's max_storage_buffer_binding_size; shaders that don't declare it still run); the compiler now declares @group(0) @binding(1) var<storage, read_write> spiral_heap: array<u32> instead of the 64 KB private array; single-flight Debug 21:23: all 9 union/array samples match C on the new runner; remaining: elements that are several values (tuples) and bounds checks; 21:35 step 4: elements made of several values (tuples): words of each component in sequence; a multi-value read assigns each target (or returns the tuple struct from a tail); samples/wgsl_array_tuple ((i32 * bool) array, runtime index): spiral-wgsl 121 = C 121 (single-flight Debug 21:35); on the Wgsl list; remaining: bounds checks (naga clamps today) and a hopac build; 22:24 BLESSED: candidate single-flight-20261012-214348 reviewed (all existing rows parity; 32 new: 11 Wgsl rows agree with C, new samples' Rust/Delphi agree except wgsl_array_tuple Delphi build-fail -> DELPHI-TUPLE-ARRAYS, 6 FOR-D repros), -Bless run single-flight-20261012-220449 reproduces it row for row (3,825 rows, 0 differences), EXPECTED.tsv 3,898 -> 3,929 rows (+31, 0 removed, 0 changed; the runaway timeout row keeps its entry); pre-bless copy tmp/compiler-main/EXPECTED-before-bless-2.tsv; 01:38 BLESSED pack-0002: -Bless run single-flight-20261013-011643 reproduces the reviewed candidate single-flight-20261013-001436 row for row (3,830 rows, 0 differences); EXPECTED.tsv 3,929 -> 3,935 (+6: runaway_inline_match_recursion C error, wgsl_gadt_payload_union x5; 30 changed: 2 GADT C rows error -> ok exit 0, wgsl_array_tuple Delphi build-fail -> 121, Gleam residuals from the spiral_array_to_list prelude); hopac-20261013-003348 had the identical delta; pre-bless copy tmp/compiler-main/EXPECTED-before-bless-3.tsv; 2026-10-13 23:42 closed in the near-done sweep: the gpt5parallel build it waited on is done (hopac-20261013-003348 had pack-0002's identical delta); bounds checks are not added: array index/update is unchecked on C (the oracle), Kotlin and Swift too, so WGSL clamping is within the same contract and a checked index would be a cross-backend decision, not a WGSL gap", TaskKind, P1, D5, 100u32, Done, "WGSL-PAYLOAD-UNIONS", "wgsl,compiler,ml", "current", "current")
